### Comprehensive Study of JDBC Architecture, Driver Types & Database Programming

#### 1. The JDBC Architectural Model:
```
       +---------------------------------------------+
       |            Java Application Code            |
       +---------------------------------------------+
                              |
                              v
       +---------------------------------------------+
       |             JDBC API (java.sql)             |
       |  Connection, Statement, ResultSet, Driver   |
       +---------------------------------------------+
                              |
                              v
       +---------------------------------------------+
       |        DriverManager / DataSource           |
       +---------------------------------------------+
                              |
                              v
       +---------------------------------------------+
       |           JDBC Driver (Types 1-4)           |
       +---------------------------------------------+
                              |
                              v
       +---------------------------------------------+
       |          Relational Database Engine         |
       |         (MySQL, Oracle, PostgreSQL)         |
       +---------------------------------------------+
```

#### 2. Detailed Technical Comparison of the 4 Driver Types:
| Parameter | Type 1: JDBC-ODBC Bridge | Type 2: Native-API Driver | Type 3: Network-Protocol Driver | Type 4: Pure Java Thin Driver |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Purity** | Mixed (Java + ODBC + C) | Mixed (Java + Native client C) | All Java (Client & Middleware) | **100% Pure Java** |
| **Speed** | Slowest (double translation) | Moderate | Moderate (middleware hop) | **Fastest** (direct socket IO) |
| **Client Install**| Requires ODBC setup | Requires vendor native DB client | Zero client configuration | **Zero install** (bundled JAR) |
| **Portability** | Non-portable (OS dependent) | Non-portable | Highly portable | **Most portable** |

#### 3. Complete Demonstrative Java Implementation: Database Creation, Table & Query:
```java
import java.sql.*;

public class JdbcApplicationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/";
        String user = "root";
        String pass = "password";

        System.out.println("--- Starting JDBC Demonstration ---");

        // Standard Step-by-Step JDBC Workflow
        try {
            // Step 1: Register JDBC Driver
            Class.forName("com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver");
            System.out.println("Step 1: MySQL JDBC Driver registered successfully.");

            // Step 2: Establish Database Connection
            try (Connection conn = DriverManager.getConnection(url, user, pass);
                 Statement stmt = conn.createStatement()) {

                System.out.println("Step 2: Connected to database server successfully.");

                // Step 3: Create a New Database
                String createDbSql = "CREATE DATABASE IF NOT EXISTS mgit_exam_db";
                stmt.executeUpdate(createDbSql);
                System.out.println("Step 3: Database 'mgit_exam_db' verified / created.");

                // Switch to new database
                stmt.execute("USE mgit_exam_db");

                // Step 4: Create Table
                String createTableSql = "CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS students ("
                        + "roll_no INT PRIMARY KEY, "
                        + "name VARCHAR(50), "
                        + "marks DOUBLE"
                        + ")";
                stmt.executeUpdate(createTableSql);
                System.out.println("Step 4: Table 'students' created successfully.");

                // Step 5: Insert and Query Records
                stmt.executeUpdate("INSERT INTO students VALUES (101, 'Ananya', 94.5)");
                ResultSet rs = stmt.executeQuery("SELECT * FROM students");

                System.out.println("\n--- Query Results ---");
                while (rs.next()) {
                    System.out.printf("Roll No: %d | Name: %-10s | Marks: %.2f\n",
                            rs.getInt("roll_no"),
                            rs.getString("name"),
                            rs.getDouble("marks"));
                }
                rs.close();
            }

        } catch (ClassNotFoundException e) {
            System.err.println("JDBC Driver not found: " + e.getMessage());
        } catch (SQLException e) {
            System.err.println("SQL Execution Exception: " + e.getMessage());
        }

        System.out.println("--- JDBC Resources Closed Deterministically ---");
    }
}
```

$$\therefore \text{Type 4 thin drivers eliminate client configuration and provide high-speed native socket connectivity.}$$
